      "source": "run moe_stage2-ch16h, 2026-08-12, 16h budget, step-1 reachability check",
      "title": "Reachability check beats a loose parity gate",
      "lever": "When a loose correctness tolerance seems to license a narrower B format, first trace the harness's pinned quant mode and scale granularity through the heuristic dispatch to the exact template instance, and check the codegen admits that dtype pair.",
      "apply": "Follow scale granularity (per-1x128 fp32 vs per-1x32 E8M0 MX) and A/B dtypes into the device-op selection: the two live on separate frozen gridwise templates, and the generator ties B's dtype to A's except for one integer-4 case, so the mixed pair is never emitted.",
      "verify": "Compile an #error canary into the file you believe you are editing and confirm the build breaks; if it compiles clean and outputs stay bit-exact, that tree is a decorative mirror and the JIT include path reads a different one.",
      "pitfall": "A mirrored source tree looked like the modifiable surface -> the canary compiled clean and every case stayed bit-exact -> the edits belong in the codegen tree the JIT include path actually consumes.",
      "caution": "Also verify the traffic cut lands on the critical path: on a dependency-latency-bound dequant/accumulate/epilogue chain the extra unpack work can absorb what the narrower operand saves."
